<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=anji-bridge">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anji Bridge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">595–<a href="/years/605">605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Zhaozhou Bridge, Great Stone Bridge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anji Bridge, or Zhaozhou Bridge, is a stone bridge over the Xiaohe River in Zhao County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, built in 595–605 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> and credited to the craftsman Li Chun; it is the world's oldest open-spandrel segmental arch bridge of stone. Its shallow arch spans 37.37 metres and saves about 40 per cent of the material a semicircular arch would need. Two small arches at each end lighten it and let floodwater through. It is the oldest standing bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and influenced later Chinese bridges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anji-bridge.jpg" alt="Anji Bridge" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Siyuwj · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anji_Bridge,_Zhao_County,_2020-09-06_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>From 581 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> reasserted imperial control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and undertook immense public works, among them two imperial capitals and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. Goods carried overland south and south-west from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> had to cross the Xiao River near Luanzhou, later Zhaozhou, which flowed through a low-lying plain and hindered continuous movement by land. It was here that Li Chun, who directed masons and other craftsmen, built the bridge. Chinese history credits him with its design, but no contemporary record of the work survives, and later chronicles mention it only briefly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The bridge is about 64 metres long, 7.3 metres tall and 9 metres wide. Its arch covers a segment of 84 degrees with a radius of 27.27 metres, giving a rise-to-span ratio of about 0.197, far below the 0.5 of a semicircular arch. The central arch is made of 28 thin, curved limestone slabs joined with iron butterfly joints, so that it can adjust to shifts in its supports and stand even if one segment breaks. The four side arches reduce its weight by about 15.3 per cent, or roughly 700 tons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> officials left an inscription on it seventy years after its construction praising Li Chun's work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The bridge influenced later designs such as the Yongtong Bridge near Zhao County, a stone segmental-arch bridge 26 metres long built in 1130 by Pou Qianer. Legends credited it to Lu Ban, said to have built it in a single night. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> authors likened it to a new moon rising above the clouds, but it later fell into obscurity until Liang Sicheng of Tsinghua University studied and measured it in the 1930s. Severely damaged in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, it was renovated in 1952 and 1956 in ways criticised for changing its appearance. It became an International Historic Civil Engineering Landmark in 1991 and was nominated for the World Heritage List in 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anji Bridge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>595–605</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Zhao County, Hebei, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Li Chun · <a href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>No record from the time of its building survives; the credit to Li Chun comes from later chronicles.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/arch">Arch<span>before 3800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=anji-bridge">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
